s New Roman&quot;;" show="Name and value" paramname="R" paramvalue="10KΩ">R=10KΩ</text></g><g x="105" y="280" transform="translate(105,280)" width="25" height="15" class="part" name="part" xo="0" yo="0" sref="X1" directory="std"><g width="150" top="572" left="0" height="150" zoom="20" symbol="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;GND&quot;,&quot;reference&quot;:&quot;X&quot;,&quot;device&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;std&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;GND&quot;}}"></g><polyline points="9,12 21,12 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: #2196f3; fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><polyline points="13,14 17,14 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: #2196f3; fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><g points="15,5 15,10" class="polyline" name="pin" type="simple" netid="5" netidpos="0" netId="5" netIdPos="0"><polyline points="15,5 15,10 " style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline><rect width="6" height="6" class="pin" x="12" y="2" style="stroke: rgb(0, 255, 0); fill: none; stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></rect><text r="0" x="13" y="12" transform="rotate(90 13 12)" style="font-size: 12px; font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; display: none;">p</text><text r="0" x="15" y="5" transform="rotate(0 15 5)" style="font-size: 12px; font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; display: none;"> </text><ellipse cx="15" cy="8.5" rx="1.5" ry="1.5" style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); fill: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></ellipse><polygon points="13.5,10 16.5,10 15,11.5 " style="stroke: rgb(255, 0, 0); fill: rgb(255, 0, 0); stroke-width: 1px; display: none;"></polygon></g><polyline points="7,10 23,10 " class="polyline" name="polyline" style="stroke: #2196f3; fill: none; stroke-width: 1px;"></polyline></g><g transform="translate(800,20)" x="800" y="20" title="codeHTML" width="340" height="520" class="codeHTML" name="codeHTML" rot="0"><foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="340" height="520" style="height: 520px; width: 340px;"><div name="htmlCode" style="zoom: 0.34; height: 1529.41px; width: 1000px;" code="&lt;div class=&quot;ch&quot;&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;Inverting Schmitt Trigger with an Op-Amp&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Overview&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;A Schmitt trigger is a voltage comparator with &lt;strong&gt;positive feedback&lt;/strong&gt;, so it has &lt;strong&gt;two different switching thresholds&lt;/strong&gt; instead of one. The gap between them is called &lt;strong&gt;hysteresis&lt;/strong&gt;. It keeps the output from chattering when the input is slow or noisy near a threshold. This circuit turns the sine wave at the input into a &lt;strong&gt;clean square wave&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Circuit Components&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;ul style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vin:&lt;/strong&gt; sine source, 6 V amplitude at 1 kHz, connected to the inverting input (&amp;minus;) of the op-amp.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;U1:&lt;/strong&gt; op-amp with saturation voltages Vcc = +12 V and Vee = &amp;minus;12 V.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R1 = 20 K&amp;Omega;:&lt;/strong&gt; feedback resistor from the output (out) to the non-inverting input (+).&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R2 = 10 K&amp;Omega;:&lt;/strong&gt; resistor from the (+) input to ground. Together with R1 it forms a divider that creates the threshold voltage p.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nodes:&lt;/strong&gt; in (input), p (threshold voltage), out (output), 0 (ground).&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;Threshold Calculation&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-formula&quot;&gt;
        $$\beta=\dfrac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}=\dfrac{10}{20+10}=\dfrac{1}{3}$$
        $$V_{UTP}=+\beta\,V_{sat}=+4\ \mathrm{V},\qquad V_{LTP}=-\beta\,V_{sat}=-4\ \mathrm{V}$$
        $$V_H=V_{UTP}-V_{LTP}=2\beta\,V_{sat}=8\ \mathrm{V}$$
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;How the Circuit Works&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-grid&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col blue&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Output High (+12 V)&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The (+) input voltage is p = +4 V.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The output stays high as long as Vin is below +4 V.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;When Vin rises above &lt;strong&gt;+4 V&lt;/strong&gt; the differential input turns negative and the output switches to &amp;minus;12 V.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col red&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Output Low (&amp;minus;12 V)&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The (+) input voltage is p = &amp;minus;4 V.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The output stays low as long as Vin is above &amp;minus;4 V.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;When Vin falls below &lt;strong&gt;&amp;minus;4 V&lt;/strong&gt; the output switches back to +12 V.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6;&quot;&gt;Because the input is applied to the inverting terminal, the circuit is &lt;strong&gt;inverting&lt;/strong&gt;: the output is a square wave in opposition to the input. The &lt;strong&gt;p&lt;/strong&gt; curve in the plot is the threshold voltage itself, and it toggles between +4 V and &amp;minus;4 V at every switching event.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;Simulation Results (Transient, 0 - 3 ms)&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-grid&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col blue&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Switching&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Switches low when Vin &amp;asymp; &lt;strong&gt;+4.1 V&lt;/strong&gt; (for example at 0.12 ms).&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Switches high when Vin &amp;asymp; &lt;strong&gt;&amp;minus;4.1 V&lt;/strong&gt; (for example at 0.62 ms).&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The small excess above 4 V comes from the 5 &amp;micro;s simulation step and the finite response time of the op-amp.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col red&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Output&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Square wave between &lt;strong&gt;+11.97 V&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&amp;minus;11.97 V&lt;/strong&gt; (close to &amp;plusmn;12 V).&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Period of 1 ms, the same frequency as the input (1 kHz).&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;The voltage p swings between &lt;strong&gt;+3.99 V&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&amp;minus;3.99 V&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card green-border&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Conclusion&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;The circuit converts any signal that crosses &amp;plusmn;4 V into a &amp;plusmn;12 V square wave, and it ignores any ripple or noise smaller than the hysteresis gap (8 V). The hysteresis width depends only on the resistor ratio and the saturation voltage, not on the input frequency. Typical applications include noise removal, switch debouncing, level detection and relaxation oscillators.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Try It Yourself&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;ul style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;Run the &lt;strong&gt;transient analysis&lt;/strong&gt; and compare the in, out and p curves.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;Change R1 to 10 K&amp;Omega; so the thresholds become &amp;plusmn;6 V: a 6 V input barely makes the output switch.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;Reduce the amplitude of Vin to 3 V (below the 4 V threshold) and the output never changes. This is the effect of hysteresis.&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;Make R1 very small to enlarge the thresholds, or very large to shrink them toward an ordinary comparator.&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
"> 
